CAT = "txadjValuation"; SEG = "VAL"
Q = [
("A roof costs $18,000 to replace. It has a 20-year expected life and is 12 years old. Its actual cash value is:",
 "$7,200", ["$10,800", "$18,000", "$6,000"],
 "Depreciation is 12 / 20, or 60 percent, of $18,000, which is $10,800. Actual cash value is $18,000 - $10,800 = $7,200."),
("The price a willing buyer would pay a willing seller, neither being under pressure to act, is:",
 "Fair market value", ["Actual cash value", "Replacement cost", "Agreed value"],
 "Fair market value is what a willing buyer and willing seller would agree on. Actual cash value is usually replacement cost less depreciation, though courts may consider market value."),
("A building worth $800,000 is insured for $480,000 under an 80 percent coinsurance clause. A fire causes $100,000 of damage, and the deductible is $1,000. The insurer pays:",
 "$74,000", ["$75,000", "$59,000", "$99,000"],
 "The insured needed $640,000 (80 percent of $800,000). Payment is $480,000 / $640,000 x $100,000 = $75,000, less the $1,000 deductible = $74,000."),
("A building worth $500,000 is insured for $460,000 under a 90 percent coinsurance clause. A $30,000 loss occurs and the deductible is $2,500. The insurer pays:",
 "$27,500", ["$30,000", "$25,000", "$24,750"],
 "The requirement is $450,000, and the insured carried $460,000, so there is no penalty. The loss less the deductible is $27,500."),
("A building worth $600,000 is insured for $300,000 under an 80 percent coinsurance clause and is totally destroyed. The insurer pays:",
 "$300,000", ["$375,000", "$480,000", "$240,000"],
 "The coinsurance formula gives $300,000 / $480,000 x $600,000 = $375,000, but the insurer never pays more than the limit of insurance, $300,000."),
("Policy A ($150,000) and Policy B ($250,000) both cover a building with pro rata other insurance clauses. On an $80,000 loss, Policy A pays:",
 "$30,000", ["$40,000", "$50,000", "$80,000"],
 "Policy A's share is $150,000 / $400,000, or 37.5 percent, of $80,000, which is $30,000. Equal shares would give $40,000."),
("A primary policy with a $100,000 limit and an excess policy cover the same loss of $140,000. The excess policy pays:",
 "$40,000", ["$70,000", "$56,000", "$140,000"],
 "The primary policy pays first up to its $100,000 limit. The excess policy then pays the remaining $40,000."),
("A home with a $250,000 Coverage A limit has a 2 percent wind and hail deductible. On a $14,000 hail claim, the insurer pays:",
 "$9,000", ["$13,720", "$12,000", "$5,000"],
 "A percentage deductible is figured on the Coverage A limit, not the loss: 2 percent of $250,000 is $5,000, and $14,000 - $5,000 = $9,000."),
("A classic car insured on an agreed value basis for $60,000 is stolen and never recovered. The insurer pays:",
 "The agreed $60,000", ["Its market value that day", "Its cost new less wear", "Whatever a dealer quotes"],
 "Agreed value coverage fixes the amount payable on a total loss when the policy is written, so no valuation dispute arises after the loss."),
("A storm-damaged vessel would cost more to recover and repair than it would be worth afterward. This is a:",
 "Constructive total loss", ["Partial loss", "General average loss", "Particular average loss"],
 "A constructive total loss exists when recovery and repair would cost more than the property's value after repair, so it is settled as a total loss."),
("After paying a total loss on a car, the insurer sells the wreck at auction. The money it recovers is:",
 "Salvage", ["Subrogation", "Contribution", "Depreciation"],
 "Salvage is the value recovered from damaged property the insurer takes after paying a total loss. Subrogation is recovery from a responsible third party."),
("After a $9,000 total loss settlement, the insured wants to keep the damaged car, which has a salvage value of $1,500. The insurer will usually pay:",
 "$7,500", ["$9,000", "$10,500", "$6,000"],
 "When the owner keeps the salvage, the settlement is reduced by its value: $9,000 - $1,500 = $7,500."),
("A dispute decided by a neutral third party whose decision the parties agreed in advance to accept, and which can cover liability as well as amount, is:",
 "Binding arbitration", ["Appraisal", "Mediation", "Subrogation"],
 "Appraisal decides only the amount of loss. Mediation is non-binding. Binding arbitration can resolve broader issues, such as fault, when the parties agree to it."),
("An older home's room layout and outdated wiring lower its value even though nothing is worn out. This loss in value is:",
 "Functional obsolescence", ["Physical depreciation", "Economic obsolescence", "Recoverable depreciation"],
 "Functional obsolescence is loss of value from outdated design or features. Economic obsolescence comes from outside the property, such as a change in the neighborhood."),
("A store loses value because a new highway draws traffic away from it. This is:",
 "Economic obsolescence", ["Functional obsolescence", "Physical wear and tear", "Betterment"],
 "Economic (external) obsolescence is loss of value from conditions outside the property, such as traffic patterns, zoning or the local economy."),
("On a replacement cost claim, the depreciation the insurer withholds until repairs are completed is called:",
 "Recoverable depreciation", ["Non-recoverable depreciation", "Salvage value", "Betterment"],
 "Recoverable depreciation is the holdback between actual cash value and replacement cost, paid once the insured repairs or replaces the property."),
("An auto claim is reduced because the new tires installed in the repair are much better than the worn tires they replaced. This is a charge for:",
 "Betterment", ["Salvage", "Coinsurance", "Subrogation"],
 "Betterment is the improvement in value when new parts replace worn ones. Charging for it keeps the insured from profiting from the loss."),
("A deductible that applies to all losses combined during the policy year, rather than to each loss, is a(n):",
 "Aggregate deductible", ["Per-occurrence deductible", "Franchise deductible", "Percentage deductible"],
 "An aggregate deductible is satisfied once total retained losses for the year reach it. After that, losses are paid in full."),
("Under a $1,000 franchise deductible, a $3,500 covered loss is paid:",
 "$3,500", ["$2,500", "$1,000", "$0"],
 "A franchise deductible pays nothing on losses below the threshold and pays the whole loss once the threshold is exceeded."),
("Replacement cost means the cost to repair or replace the damaged property with:",
 "New of like kind and quality", ["The cheapest available item", "An item of the same age", "Upgraded, modern materials"],
 "Replacement cost is the cost to repair or replace with materials of like kind and quality, with no deduction for depreciation and no upgrade."),
("Under the broad evidence rule, an adjuster setting actual cash value may consider:",
 "Every relevant fact about value", ["Only replacement cost less wear", "Only the purchase receipt", "Only the tax assessed value"],
 "The broad evidence rule lets every fact that logically bears on value be weighed, including replacement cost, depreciation, market value and obsolescence."),
("Whether an insured met a coinsurance requirement is judged by the property's value at the time of:",
 "The loss", ["Policy inception", "The purchase", "The last renewal"],
 "Coinsurance compares the limit carried with the required percentage of value at the time of loss, which is why inflation can create a penalty."),
("Coverage written on a stated amount basis pays a total loss at:",
 "Lesser of stated amount or ACV", ["The stated amount, always", "Replacement cost, always", "Market value, plus 10 percent"],
 "Unlike agreed value, a stated amount is only a cap: the insurer pays the lesser of the stated amount or actual cash value."),
("A co-owner with a half interest insures a $200,000 building in her own name only, for $200,000, and it is totally destroyed. The most the policy pays her is:",
 "$100,000", ["$200,000", "$150,000", "$50,000"],
 "Recovery is limited to the insured's insurable interest. Paying her the whole value would put her in a better position than before the loss."),
("Two policies on the same property each say they are excess over any other insurance. Courts commonly resolve this by:",
 "Sharing the loss pro rata", ["Making the older one primary", "Paying nothing under either", "Letting the insured pick one"],
 "Conflicting excess clauses are usually found mutually repugnant and disregarded, so the policies share the loss in proportion to their limits."),
("A signed, sworn proof of loss typically states:",
 "The time, cause and amount of loss", ["The adjuster's license number", "The agent's commission earned", "The premium owed next year"],
 "A proof of loss sets out the time and cause of the loss, the insured's interest, other insurance, encumbrances and the amount claimed."),
("A fence with a replacement cost of $6,000 is 40 percent depreciated. With a $500 deductible, the actual cash value payment is:",
 "$3,100", ["$3,600", "$5,500", "$1,900"],
 "Actual cash value is $6,000 x 60 percent = $3,600, and $3,600 - $500 = $3,100."),
("A home insured for $180,000 on a replacement cost basis is destroyed, and rebuilding will cost $230,000. Without an extended replacement cost endorsement, the insurer pays at most:",
 "$180,000", ["$230,000", "$200,000", "$144,000"],
 "Replacement cost coverage still stops at the limit of insurance. An extended replacement cost endorsement would add a percentage above the limit."),
]
